  "statement": "A `k x k` **magic square** is a `k x k` grid filled with integers such that every row sum, every column sum, and both diagonal sums are **all equal**. The integers in the magic square **do not have to be distinct**. Every `1 x 1` grid is trivially a **magic square**.\n\nGiven an `m x n` integer `grid`, return _the **size** (i.e., the side length_ `k`_) of the **largest magic square** that can be found within this grid_.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n![](./assets/largest-magic-square-1.jpg)\n```\nInput: grid = [[7,1,4,5,6],[2,5,1,6,4],[1,5,4,3,2],[1,2,7,3,4]]\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: The largest magic square has a size of 3.\nEvery row sum, column sum, and diagonal sum of this magic square is equal to 12.\n- Row sums: 5+1+6 = 5+4+3 = 2+7+3 = 12\n- Column sums: 5+5+2 = 1+4+7 = 6+3+3 = 12\n- Diagonal sums: 5+4+3 = 6+4+2 = 12\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n![](./assets/largest-magic-square-2.jpg)\n```\nInput: grid = [[5,1,3,1],[9,3,3,1],[1,3,3,8]]\nOutput: 2\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `m == grid.length`\n*   `n == grid[i].length`\n*   `1 <= m, n <= 50`\n*   `1 <= grid[i][j] <= 10^(6)`",
  "constraints": [
    "m == grid.length",
    "n == grid[i].length",
    "1 <= m, n <= 50",
    "1 <= grid[i][j] <= 106"
  ],
